# Claude Code Tooling in the chezmoi Dotfiles Repo

This machine's Claude Code skills, MCP servers, plugins, and CLAUDE.md are managed by the
chezmoi dotfiles repo at `{{ $repoRoot }}` (source tree at `{{ $sourceDir }}`) — regardless of
which project or repo you're currently working in, that is where the guidance below applies.
This machine may run multiple named Claude Code profiles ("personas") in addition to the
unnamed default, each with its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` — the guidance below refers to them as
"personas" throughout, whether or not any are currently configured on this machine.
Diagnosing installation health, editing settings/plugins, or reasoning about skill/MCP usage
(e.g. `/doctor`) concerns that repo's `{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/`,
`{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoidata/`, and `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/`, not the current
working directory.

## Two Distinct Sources of Skills

**Native skills** (`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/skills/`): authored and version-controlled in
the chezmoi repo above. `ls` there is authoritative over any hand-maintained list — same
anti-drift principle as `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/`, where `ls` is likewise
authoritative over any hand-maintained table.
Deployed by `chezmoi apply` to `~/.claude/skills/`, symlinked from each persona's
`~/.claude-<name>/skills` (see "Personas Share Skills, Rules, and CLAUDE.md via Symlink" below).

**External skills**: declared in `packages.darwin.ai.agents.claude_code.skills` in
`{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoidata/packages.yaml`, fetched from third-party GitHub repos
(e.g. `basicmachines-co/basic-memory-skills`, `specstoryai/agent-skills`) by
`{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_darwin-37-install-claude-skills.sh.tmpl`.
Their source of truth is the external repo, not the chezmoi repo — adding, removing, or pinning
one is a `packages.yaml` edit, and their files won't be found in the chezmoi repo itself. They
land in the same `~/.claude/skills/` directory as the native ones, so `~/.claude/skills/` on
disk is a merge of both sources.

## MCP Servers and Plugins

Also declared under `packages.darwin.ai.agents.claude_code` in that same `packages.yaml`:

- `mcp_servers` — installed by
  `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_darwin-38-install-claude-mcp-servers.sh.tmpl`
- `plugin_marketplaces` / `plugins` — installed by
  `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_darwin-39-install-claude-plugins.sh.tmpl`,
  which runs `claude plugins install <id> --scope user` per entry; that CLI command is what
  actually records `enabledPlugins: true` in `~/.claude/settings.json`, not the script directly

## Disabling, Removing, or Overriding Requires a packages.yaml Cross-Check (e.g. /doctor audits)

A skill/MCP-server/plugin usage audit (`/doctor` Check 1, or similar) needs a `packages.yaml`
cross-check before any removal is proposed. Declared items — everything under
`packages.darwin.ai.agents.claude_code` above — are managed machine-wide by this repo; a local
`skillOverrides: off` or `disabledMcpServers` entry doesn't undo that, it just drifts from the
declared state. Zero/stale usage on a declared item is a signal to edit `packages.yaml`
(remove/replace the declaration), not to disable it locally. The `clean-claude-orphans` Skill
(`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/skills/clean-claude-orphans/`) already draws exactly this
distinction — it has `disable-model-invocation: true`, so it only runs when invoked via the
Skill tool, for anything genuinely installed-but-undeclared; its `SKILL.md` documents the same
logic for applying it by hand.

## Checking for Override Drift (the mirror-image case)

`clean-claude-orphans` handles installed-but-undeclared drift. This is the opposite
direction — **declared-but-locally-suppressed**. `check-claude-overrides` detects it, and is
relevant whenever auditing tooling health (`/doctor`-style checks) or when a skill/plugin isn't
behaving despite being installed and declared — it checks every persona's live `settings.json`
against that persona's own `modify_settings.json.tmpl` baseline (the
`$extra`/`claudeExtraSettings` dict, if any, passed to
`includeTemplate "claude-settings-hooks-modifier"`) and flags:

- `skillOverrides.<skill>: "off"` for a **native** skill (listed under
  `{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/skills/`) the baseline doesn't explain — someone toggled it
  off ad hoc with `/skill` in a session and it was never captured in source.
- `enabledPlugins.<id>: false` for a plugin that's in `packages.yaml`'s `claude_code.plugins`
  list (meaning chezmoi intends it enabled) the baseline doesn't explain — same ad hoc drift,
  opposite direction.

Each flagged entry needs the user's input on which way it should go:

- **Keep the override** → `check-claude-overrides --fix <persona>
  <skillOverrides|enabledPlugins> <key>` reads the live value straight from
  `settings.json`, writes it into that persona's `modify_settings.json.tmpl` `$extra` dict,
  and verifies the write (via a re-render) before the tracked file is ever touched —
  hand-editing the dict directly isn't the supported path.
- **Drop the override** → removing the key via the equivalent `/skill` or `/plugin` command (or
  editing the live `settings.json` directly) is correct, since nothing in chezmoi asked for it.
  This direction stays manual — `check-claude-overrides` never modifies a live `settings.json`.

## Personas Share Skills, Rules, and CLAUDE.md via Symlink

Each persona (`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude-<name>/`) gets `symlink_skills.tmpl`,
`symlink_rules.tmpl`, and `symlink_CLAUDE.md.tmpl` pointing back at `~/.claude/skills`,
`~/.claude/rules`, and `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` — so a skill edit, a rule edit, or a
`CLAUDE.md.tmpl` edit takes effect for every persona after one `chezmoi apply`, with no
per-persona copy to keep in sync.

**Not a "local" file, despite the path**: `~/.claude*/CLAUDE.md` is this symlink — checked-in
content sourced from `{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/CLAUDE.md.tmpl`, not personal/local
content. Any check that distinguishes "LOCAL/personal" CLAUDE.md from "checked-in" (e.g. a
`/doctor`-style dedup pass) classifies it as checked-in. A genuinely personal,
non-chezmoi-managed layer only exists if the user separately created a `CLAUDE.local.md`
alongside it, and its actual presence — not an assumption — is what confirms that.

Being checked-in doesn't mean uneditable — it means edits go through the source, the same
principle as `packages.yaml`. If `/doctor` (or any diagnosis) concludes `~/.claude*/CLAUDE.md`
should change, the edit target is `{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/CLAUDE.md.tmpl`, not the symlink;
a commit there propagates on the next `chezmoi apply` (the user's own or any other persona's).
The same applies to any skill or rule under `{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/` (`skills/`, `rules/`):
the source file there is the edit target, not the deployed copy under `~/.claude/`.

What's **not** shared per persona: `settings.json`, `.claude.json`, `plugins/`, and session
history (`projects/`). `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json` is the correct reference for all of
these, not the bare `~/.claude.json`, which belongs to a different persona and won't reflect
(or affect) the active one. Each persona's `modify_settings.json.tmpl` chezmoi-manages a
baseline `skillOverrides`/`enabledPlugins`/`permissions` posture for that persona's typical
workload (see the comment at the top of
`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude-personal/modify_settings.json.tmpl` — these postures are derived
from `/doctor` runs per-persona, and copying them verbatim between personas skips re-running
that analysis for a different workload).

**Drift note**: ad hoc `/skill`/`/plugin` toggles made directly in a session survive `chezmoi
apply` — a `modify_` script only enforces the specific keys it sets, it doesn't strip
additions — so the live `~/.claude*/settings.json` can carry more `skillOverrides`/
`enabledPlugins` entries than the chezmoi template declares. "Checking for Override Drift"
above covers how to find and resolve these.

## Two Deliberately Different Install Models

- **`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/skills/` + `packages.yaml`** (this file's subject): global,
  always-on, identical across every persona and project on this machine.
- **The `chezmoi-personal` marketplace** (`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_local/share/claude-plugins/`,
  deployed by `chezmoi apply` to `~/.local/share/claude-plugins`): chezmoi-managed like any
  other `home/` content — its path is declared as a plain entry in `packages.yaml`'s
  `plugin_marketplaces` list and registered by the same generic
  `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoiscripts/run_onchange_after_darwin-39-install-claude-plugins.sh.tmpl`
  every other declared marketplace goes through (no dedicated script). What makes it
  different from the bullet above isn't its registration mechanism — it's that declaring the
  marketplace only makes its plugins *installable*; which plugins are actually enabled for a
  given project stays a deliberate, per-project decision (`/plugin install`), not a
  machine-wide one. Adding something here just because it's useful somewhere skips the
  marketplace's actual purpose, which exists for exactly that per-project case.
